  <main>
    <p class="lead"><b>RealAether is self-funded citizen science run on home hardware.</b>
      It's built by Geebs &amp; 3TH3R3AL1 on Raspberry Pi computers in South Carolina. We watch the sky, the Sun and the Earth,
      and we ask questions that are usually dismissed or never tested. Then we test them in public with open data, rules
      we set before we look at the results, and honest reporting, including when the answer is "nothing there."</p>

    <h2>What we're exploring</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status">Live</span>
        <h3>AetherWeather</h3>
        <p>A live dashboard of cosmic and Earth weather: solar flares and the solar wind, geomagnetic activity, earthquakes,
          volcanoes, meteor showers and the ISS. The data comes from NOAA SWPC, USGS and Where The ISS At.</p>
        <p class="go"><a href="../">Open AetherWeather →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status">Live</span>
        <h3>Cosmic Ray Randomness Detector</h3>
        <p>A Raspberry Pi camera (Sony IMX708) sealed completely light-tight takes one-second dark exposures around the clock.
          Charged particles, mostly cosmic-ray muons, leave dots and streaks, and the sensor noise is harvested as random bits.
          A Hailo-10H AI accelerator fingerprints every hit image and flags the unusual ones. It isn't trained on our data,
          and a flag only means "looks different," not "something special." Space weather and logged world events are
          charted alongside, in the spirit of the Global Consciousness Project.</p>
        <p class="go"><a href="../crrd/">Open the detector →</a> · <a href="../crrd/methods.html">Methods</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status">Live · Phase 1</span>
        <h3>Impact hypothesis: NEO close-approach tracker</h3>
        <p>Upcoming near-Earth object flybys of Earth, the Moon and Mars from NASA/JPL CNEOS, the authoritative list.
          Close or well-observed approaches (470 at launch) are re-integrated independently with the REBOUND N-body code
          and the JPL DE440s ephemeris, and the differences are published, including the worst ones.</p>
        <p class="go"><a href="../impact/">Open the tracker →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status">Live · logging</span>
        <h3>Solar wind × volcano log</h3>
        <p>Every solar wind speed spike (NOAA SWPC) is logged next to every U.S. volcano alert-level change (USGS).
          A spike rule and a ±48-hour match window were fixed in advance, and the running tally is always shown against
          what chance alone would give. The record is young, and matches so far are few or none. That is reported as it is.</p>
        <p class="go"><a href="../logger/">Open the log →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Our principles</h2>
    <ul class="principles">
      <li><b>Open data and open methods.</b> Code is MIT-licensed and data and charts are CC BY 4.0, all in the public
        <a href="https://github.com/Geebs369/realaether">GitHub repository</a>. Each project has a methods section and downloadable raw files.</li>
      <li><b>Fixed rules, set in advance.</b> Thresholds, windows and tests are written down before the data comes in,
        so they can't be tuned afterward to make a pattern appear.</li>
      <li><b>Testing, not proof.</b> A match between two things is a reason to keep counting, not a discovery.
        Results are always compared with what chance would give, and correlation is never presented as cause.</li>
      <li><b>Null results get reported.</b> If a test finds nothing, the page says so and stays up.</li>
      <li><b>Authoritative sources first.</b> Official catalogs (NASA/JPL, NOAA, USGS) are the reference. Our own calculations check them
        and never replace them, and every disagreement is shown.</li>
      <li><b>Money in the open.</b> Every purchase funded by donations is posted publicly. Donations are not tax-deductible.</li>
      <li><b>Privacy.</b> The site shows no home address or home-network details. The hardware only pushes read-only snapshots out.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status plan">Planned · Phase 2</span>
        <h3>The Taurid stream</h3>
        <p>Model the debris stream of comet 2P/Encke and the Taurid-complex asteroids about 13,000 years back, toward
          the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis. Encke, 2004 TG10 and 2212 Hephaistos have the long observation records needed.
          Going that far back needs a longer-range planetary model than the DE440s ephemeris, which only covers 1849–2150.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status plan">Planned · Phase 3</span>
        <h3>The Moon and Mars</h3>
        <p>Run the same streams against the Moon and Mars, and compare them with NASA lunar impact-flash records,
          LRO new-crater data and the Robbins Mars crater catalog.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="status plan">Planned · Phase 4</span>
        <h3>Monuments, sky, myth and ice</h3>
        <p>Rebuild the sky, including the precession of the Earth's axis, over Göbekli Tepe and Giza at key dates, with a
          random-date control test, including the Sweatman &amp; Tsikritsis 2017 "Vulture Stone" claim. We'll set myth and
          ice-core records of past solar activity against the same timelines. Then we'll analyze the solar wind × volcano log against chance.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>How to help</h2>
    <p>RealAether is paid for out of pocket. Donations cover hardware, electricity, internet and computing time.
      Sharing the pages, checking our methods and telling us where we're wrong helps just as much.</p>
